window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][35]={"university_physics_en:M35-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M35-L1 – Band Structure, Fermi Surfaces and Effective Mass<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M35 – Master: Advanced Solid-State Physics and Materials Science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14: crystals and band model; M12:\nquantum mechanics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the width of a\none-dimensional tight-binding band and distinguish this model from real\ncrystals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Electrons in a periodic crystal potential form Bloch states labelled\nby crystal momentum within the first Brillouin zone. A band gives\nallowed single-particle energies versus that momentum. The Fermi surface\nis the constant-energy boundary of occupied states at zero temperature,\nnot the physical outer surface of a crystal. In one dimension it reduces\nto Fermi points. Gaps and band filling help distinguish metallic from\ninsulating behavior.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a periodic chain with lattice spacing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none orbital per unit cell and real nearest-neighbor hopping\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe tight-binding model yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(k)=E_0-2t\\cos(ka)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe bandwidth is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbetween\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=\\pi/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDifferentiating gives group velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_g=(1/\\hbar)\\partial E/\\partial k=2ta\\sin(ka)/\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLocal effective mass reflects curvature:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/m^*=(1/\\hbar^2)\\partial^2E/\\partial k^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nnear a band maximum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be negative, motivating the hole picture.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(k)=E_0-2t\\cos(ka),\\qquad W=4t.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(0)=-2\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(\\pi/2a)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(\\pi/a)=+2\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving bandwidth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=4\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure plots the symmetric dispersion for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\pi/a\\le k\\le\\pi/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand marks an illustrative Fermi level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_F=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor half filling of this ideal spin-degenerate band.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_293_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Cosine energy band from negative to positive pi over a with Fermi energy zero marked.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Cosine energy band from negative to\npositive pi over a with Fermi energy zero marked.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>One-dimensional tight-binding band at illustrative half\nfilling.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0.6\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Where does group velocity vanish in the first Brillouin zone?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the Fermi surface the outer surface of a crystal?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=2.4\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=\\pm\\pi/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nmore generally at stationary band points.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. It is a structure in momentum space determined by band filling\nand dispersion.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The one-band approximation neglects multiple orbitals, Coulomb\ncorrelations, real three-dimensional geometry and disorder. A Fermi\nsurface in an interacting metal needs additional care; this graph is a\nsingle-particle illustration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Band curvature determines carrier dynamics; the next lesson studies a\nsimple transport model. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M35&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/solid-state-physics/\">David Tong – Solid State Physics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course overview covering band structures, Fermi surfaces, electron dynamics and phonons; numerical models are original; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L1 – Band Structure, Fermi Surfaces and Effective Mass · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-511-theory-of-solids-i-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Theory of Solids I, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture-note index in advanced solid-state theory; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L1 – Band Structure, Fermi Surfaces and Effective Mass · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M35-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M35-L2 – Drude Transport, Mobility and Hall Effect<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M35 – Master: Advanced Solid-State Physics and Materials Science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M35-L1: band structure; M10:\nelectromagnetism.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate elementary Drude\nconductivity and the sign of the Hall coefficient for a single electron\ncarrier type.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Electrical conduction arises when an applied field slightly perturbs\ncarrier motion. The Drude model summarizes collisions in a\nmomentum-relaxation time. The transverse Hall field can reveal carrier\nsign and, in restricted one-band conditions, carrier density. In\nmultiband systems a single Hall number cannot generally be interpreted\nas the inverse carrier density.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For isotropic electrons of density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neffective mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand constant relaxation time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconductivity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=ne^2\\tau/m^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the electron-charge magnitude. Positive mobility is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=e\\tau/m^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn weak magnetic field for one electron-like carrier type, with a\nspecified sign convention, the Hall coefficient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_H=-1/(ne)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReal transport tensors depend on magnetic field, band anisotropy and\nmultiple carrier types.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=\\frac{ne^2\\tau}{m^*},\\qquad R_H=-\\frac1{ne}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>28<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2\\times10^{28}\\,\\mathrm{m^{-3}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=10^{-14}\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.11<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>31<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m^*=9.11\\times10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.602<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe results are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.63<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma\\approx5.63\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm{S/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.76<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu\\approx1.76\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m^2/(Vs)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3.12<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_H\\approx-3.12\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{m^3/C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure illustrates the Hall sign and field geometry, not an actual\nspecimen simulation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_074_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Rectangular sample with electron current, out-of-plane magnetic field and transverse Hall voltage.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rectangular sample with electron current,\nout-of-plane magnetic field and transverse Hall voltage.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Hall geometry in a single-band Drude approximation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an ideal one-band electron conductor?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_H=-1/(ne)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerally fail in a multiband conductor?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Negative under the stated convention.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Different electron and hole populations contribute distinct\ndensities, mobilities and field responses.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Relaxation time is a model approximation rather than a universal\nmaterial parameter. Quantum oscillations, strong fields, nanoscale\ndimensions or anomalous Hall terms require more advanced\ndescriptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Relaxation times depend in part on phonons. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M35&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/solid-state-physics/\">David Tong – Solid State Physics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course overview covering band structures, Fermi surfaces, electron dynamics and phonons; numerical models are original; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L2 – Drude Transport, Mobility and Hall Effect · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ph.nat.tum.de/cmt/teaching/solid-state-theory/\">TUM – Solid State Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course overview of transport, couplings, superconductivity and interacting electrons; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L2 – Drude Transport, Mobility and Hall Effect · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M35-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M35-L3 – Phonons, Lattice Dispersion and Electron–Phonon Coupling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M35 – Master: Advanced Solid-State Physics and Materials Science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14: lattice vibrations; M35-L2:\ntransport.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive acoustic dispersion and\nmaximum angular frequency for a monoatomic mass-and-spring chain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Atoms in a crystal oscillate collectively near equilibrium positions.\nTheir quantized normal modes are phonons. A monoatomic chain possesses\nan acoustic branch with frequency proportional to wave number at long\nwavelengths. Phonons can transport heat and scatter electrons, but a\nphonon dispersion plot alone does not determine a transport\nlifetime.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nspring constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlattice spacing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand nearest-neighbor interactions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>u<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ddot u_j=K(u_{j+1}-2u_j+u_{j-1})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe wave ansatz\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_j\\propto\\exp[i(jka-\\omega t)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega^2=(4K/m)\\sin^2(ka/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe long-wavelength sound velocity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_s=a\\sqrt{K/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder an ideal harmonic-chain approximation. Electron–phonon scattering\nrequires additional coupling parameters.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mfrac><mi>K<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega(k)=2\\sqrt{\\frac Km}\\,\\left|\\sin\\frac{ka}{2}\\right|.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>26<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K/m=10^{26}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.30\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>13<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_{\\max}=2\\times10^{13}\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the zone boundary. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ka=\\pi/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>13<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=10^{13}\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin the long-wavelength limit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_s=3.0\\times10^3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plot shows an ideal acoustic branch within the first Brillouin\nzone.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_212_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Acoustic phonon frequency versus dimensionless wave number from negative pi to positive pi.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Acoustic phonon frequency versus\ndimensionless wave number from negative pi to positive pi.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Harmonic monoatomic chain: acoustic dispersion branch.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What maximum frequency follows for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>26<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K/m=4\\times10^{26}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the mode acoustic near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can the carrier relaxation time be inferred from this curve\nalone?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>13<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_{\\max}=4\\times10^{13}\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin(ka/2)\\approx ka/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\propto|k|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. One also needs interaction matrix elements, occupations and\nallowed transitions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The harmonic one-atom chain has no damping or anharmonicity and only\none acoustic branch. Optical branches require a multi-atom basis; real\nthermal and resistive properties cannot be predicted from this spring\nchain alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Under suitable conditions electron–phonon coupling can contribute to\npairing. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M35&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/solid-state-physics/\">David Tong – Solid State Physics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course overview covering band structures, Fermi surfaces, electron dynamics and phonons; numerical models are original; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L3 – Phonons, Lattice Dispersion and Electron–Phonon Coupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ph.nat.tum.de/cmt/teaching/solid-state-theory/\">TUM – Solid State Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course overview of transport, couplings, superconductivity and interacting electrons; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L3 – Phonons, Lattice Dispersion and Electron–Phonon Coupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M35-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M35-L4 – BCS Superconductivity, Cooper Pairs and the Energy Gap<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M35 – Master: Advanced Solid-State Physics and Materials Science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14: superconductivity; M35-L3:\nphonons; M28: statistical physics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply the weak-coupling BCS gap\nratio and identify its assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A superconductor is not just a metal with an infinitely long Drude\nrelaxation time. In conventional BCS theory an effective attraction near\nthe Fermi surface produces coherent fermion pairing. A quasiparticle\nenergy gap suppresses certain low-energy single-particle excitations.\nThe condensate carries macroscopic phase coherence, although real\nsamples may display vortices, critical currents and defects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For isotropic, weak-coupling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-wave\nBCS superconductivity under its standard assumptions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.53<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\Delta(0)/(k_B T_c)\\approx3.53<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the single-quasiparticle excitation gap, while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\Delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the ideal threshold for creating two quasiparticles. The gap closes\nas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msubsup><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\to T_c^-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nits complete temperature dependence requires the self-consistent BCS gap\nequation. The factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3.53<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.53<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not universal in strong-coupling or unconventional\nsuperconductors.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.53<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{2\\Delta(0)}{k_B T_c}\\approx3.53.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal BCS superconductor with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_c=10\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.61733<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_B=8.61733\\times10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm{eV/K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe estimate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.52<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta(0)\\approx1.52\\,\\mathrm{meV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\Delta(0)\\approx3.04\\,\\mathrm{meV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows an explicitly illustrative gap-closing curve, not a\nnumerical solution of the full BCS self-consistency equation or\nexperimental data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_016_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Illustrative decreasing superconducting energy gap from zero temperature to the critical temperature.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Illustrative decreasing superconducting\nenergy gap from zero temperature to the critical\ntemperature.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Qualitative BCS gap evolution; not measured data.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the weak-coupling zero-temperature gap change when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\Delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresent in ideal BCS theory?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3.53<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.53<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot universal for all superconductors?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The idealized energy for creating two appropriate quasiparticle\nexcitations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Strong coupling, anisotropy and other pairing mechanisms can change\nthe ratio.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The relation assumes a weak-coupling isotropic BCS state. It does not\ndetermine critical fields, unconventional pairing, contact resistance or\nengineering performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>When Coulomb interactions dominate, different models are required. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M35&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-511-theory-of-solids-i-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Theory of Solids I, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture-note index in advanced solid-state theory; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L4 – BCS Superconductivity, Cooper Pairs and the Energy Gap · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ph.nat.tum.de/cmt/teaching/solid-state-theory/\">TUM – Solid State Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course overview of transport, couplings, superconductivity and interacting electrons; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L4 – BCS Superconductivity, Cooper Pairs and the Energy Gap · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M35-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M35-L5 – Hubbard Model, Strong Correlations and Superexchange<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M35 – Master: Advanced Solid-State Physics and Materials Science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M35-L1: tight binding; M12: spin;\nM28: many-body statistics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate the antiferromagnetic\nsuperexchange scale in a two-site Hubbard model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A band model favors delocalized electrons, while local Coulomb\nrepulsion penalizes double occupation. The Hubbard model represents this\ncompetition through hopping\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand onsite interaction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt half filling and large\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U/t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncharge fluctuations become costly. Virtual hopping nevertheless induces\na spin exchange interaction that favors a singlet for two localized\nneighboring spins.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The elementary Hubbard Hamiltonian is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>†<\/mo><\/msubsup><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>↑<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>↓<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=-t\\sum_{\\langle ij\\rangle,\\sigma}(c^\\dagger_{i\\sigma}c_{j\\sigma}+\\mathrm{h.c.})+U\\sum_i n_{i\\uparrow}n_{i\\downarrow}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor two sites, two electrons and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>≫<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\gg t&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsecond-order perturbation theory gives antiferromagnetic exchange\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=4t^2/U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{\\mathrm{eff}}=J(\\mathbf S_1\\cdot\\mathbf S_2-1/4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithin the singly occupied subspace. It is not a universal formula for\narbitrary compounds or dopings.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>U<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>≫<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\simeq\\frac{4t^2}{U}\\qquad(U\\gg t&gt;0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0.10\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=1.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t/U=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>0.040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\simeq0.040\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nwithin this effective two-spin model the singlet lies roughly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelow the triplets. The illustration shows the spin sectors and a\nschematic virtual doubly occupied intermediate state.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_133_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two singly occupied sites and a virtual doubly occupied intermediate state of energy cost U; exchange J.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two singly occupied sites and a virtual\ndoubly occupied intermediate state of energy cost U; exchange\nJ.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Virtual Hubbard hopping in the strong-coupling regime.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0.05\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=1\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis doubled at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=4t^2/U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot an exact general materials law?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=0.010\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is multiplied by four.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It assumes large U, half filling and a restricted single-band\nmodel.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\sim t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis perturbative estimate fails. Band structure, geometry,\ndimensionality, disorder and other exchange paths can favor different\nmagnetic or metallic phases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Band-function topology provides an additional way to classify phases.\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M35&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-511-theory-of-solids-i-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Theory of Solids I, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture-note index in advanced solid-state theory; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L5 – Hubbard Model, Strong Correlations and Superexchange · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ph.nat.tum.de/cmt/teaching/solid-state-theory/\">TUM – Solid State Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course overview of transport, couplings, superconductivity and interacting electrons; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L5 – Hubbard Model, Strong Correlations and Superexchange · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M35-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M35-L6 – SSH Chain, Chiral Symmetry and Winding Number<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M35 – Master: Advanced Solid-State Physics and Materials Science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M35-L1: tight binding; M34: quantum\ninformation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish zero and nonzero\nwinding in a specified SSH block\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>The Su–Schrieffer–Heeger chain contains two sublattice sites per unit\ncell with alternating couplings\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a suitable basis the spinless model has chiral symmetry, producing\nenergies in pairs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>±<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm E(k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts complex off-diagonal hopping traces a closed curve as crystal\nmomentum traverses the Brillouin zone. Whether that curve encloses the\norigin distinguishes gapped bulk phases as long as the protecting\nsymmetry is respected.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>We explicitly choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(k)=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;q(k)^*\\\\q(k)&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(k)=v+w e^{ika}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor real\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v,w&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts energies are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\pm(k)=\\pm|q(k)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith this basis and orientation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=(1/2\\pi)\\int_{-\\pi/a}^{\\pi/a}\\!\\partial_k\\arg q(k)\\,dk=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w&gt;v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v&gt;w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe band gap is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2|v-w|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand closes at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReversing the sign of the exponential reverses the orientation and sign\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ν<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut not the criterion of whether the origin is enclosed.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi/a}^{\\pi/a}\\partial_k\\arg q(k)\\,dk.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.6\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=1.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a bulk gap of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=1.2\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a gap of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two circles in complex\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nspace have radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand centers at real\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEdge modes additionally depend on termination and symmetry.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_274_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two closed loops in complex hopping space: one encloses the origin, the other does not.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two closed loops in complex hopping\nspace: one encloses the origin, the other does not.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>SSH winding for a specified unit cell and orientation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When does the SSH gap close for positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v,w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the bulk gap for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=1.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot guarantee boundary states against arbitrary perturbations?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Protection depends, among other things, on symmetry-preserving\nperturbations and termination.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The definition requires a specified basis, unit cell and chiral\nsymmetry. Another unit-cell convention can change the winding-number\nconvention; bulk and boundary must be compared consistently.\nSymmetry-breaking terms can remove protection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>In two spatial dimensions, Berry curvature leads to a related integer\ninvariant. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M35&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://topocondmat.org/w1-topointro/w1-assignments/\">Topology in Condensed Matter – Topics for Self-Study<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · SSH chain, dimerization and symmetry assumptions for boundary states; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L6 – SSH Chain, Chiral Symmetry and Winding Number · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://topocondmat.org/w8-general/invariants/\">Topology in Condensed Matter – Different Approaches to Topological Invariants<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chiral Bloch Hamiltonian and winding number; sign depends on convention; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L6 – SSH Chain, Chiral Symmetry and Winding Number · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M35-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M35-L7 – Berry Curvature, Gauge Convention and Chern Number<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M35 – Master: Advanced Solid-State Physics and Materials Science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M35-L6: SSH winding; M34: quantum\nstates.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Transform the Berry connection\nunder a local phase change and interpret quantized curvature flux for an\nisolated band.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Bloch eigenstates may be multiplied by a momentum-dependent local\nphase. The eigenvector phase itself is therefore not a unique\nobservable. Berry connection encodes the momentum dependence of that\nphase, while its curl in two-dimensional momentum space is\ngauge-invariant Berry curvature. For a nondegenerate band separated from\nall other bands throughout the Brillouin torus, total normalized\ncurvature flux is an integer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>With convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_j(\\mathbf k)=i\\langle u(\\mathbf k)|\\partial_{k_j}u(\\mathbf k)\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>↦<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u\\rangle\\mapsto e^{i\\chi(\\mathbf k)}|u\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none gets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>↦<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mi>χ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_j\\mapsto A_j-\\partial_{k_j}\\chi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBerry curvature is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega_{xy}=\\partial_{k_x}A_y-\\partial_{k_y}A_x=i(\\langle\\partial_xu|\\partial_yu\\rangle-\\langle\\partial_yu|\\partial_xu\\rangle)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is gauge-invariant. For an isolated occupied band\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>k<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℤ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=(1/2\\pi)\\int_{\\mathrm{BZ}}\\Omega_{xy}\\,d^2k\\in\\mathbb Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nno globally smooth periodic gauge exists; local patches are used.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>k<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℤ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathrm{BZ}}\\Omega_{xy}(\\mathbf k)\\,d^2k\\in\\mathbb Z.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Partition the Brillouin torus into four patches and prescribe a\nconstructed Berry flux of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough each patch. Their total flux is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is an illustrative flux accounting exercise, not evidence for a\nparticular material or a claim of universally constant Berry curvature.\nThe image shows the four flux contributions and the gauge issue.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_019_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four Brillouin-zone patches each carry pi over two of Berry flux; their sum gives C equal to one.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four Brillouin-zone patches each carry pi\nover two of Berry flux; their sum gives C equal to one.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustrative Berry-flux balance for an isolated band.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif the total flux is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransform under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>↦<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>χ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|u\\rangle\\mapsto e^{i\\chi}|u\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing this convention?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is one globally smooth periodic eigenvector gauge obstructed\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>↦<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mi>χ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_j\\mapsto A_j-\\partial_{k_j}\\chi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The nontrivial Chern class obstructs a single globally smooth\nperiodic gauge and requires local patches.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Quantization requires a well-defined isolated band bundle over a\nclosed Brillouin zone. Degenerate bands require a non-Abelian treatment.\nThe sign depends on orientation and convention; Hall conductance\nrequires additional physical assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Topological model parameters must be complemented by structural\ncharacterization of actual samples. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M35&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://topocondmat.org/w8-general/invariants/\">Topology in Condensed Matter – Different Approaches to Topological Invariants<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chiral Bloch Hamiltonian and winding number; sign depends on convention; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L7 – Berry Curvature, Gauge Convention and Chern Number · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-511-theory-of-solids-i-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Theory of Solids I, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture-note index in advanced solid-state theory; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L7 – Berry Curvature, Gauge Convention and Chern Number · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M35-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M35-L8 – X-Ray Diffraction, Line Broadening and Scherrer Estimation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M35 – Master: Advanced Solid-State Physics and Materials Science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M14: lattices and Bragg reflections;\nM32: measurement methods.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate an\ninstrument-corrected Scherrer crystallite-size estimate and state its\nlimitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>X-ray diffraction from periodic lattice planes produces Bragg peaks.\nPeak positions indicate lattice spacing, whereas peak widths reflect\nfinite coherent domains, microstrain, instrumental resolution and\nprofile shape. A crystallite is a coherently diffracting domain and need\nnot equal a visible particle or aggregate. The Scherrer equation only\nprovides a model-dependent size estimate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The common notation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=K_s\\lambda/(\\beta_s\\cos\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a shape/profile factor,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe wavelength,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhalf of the scattering angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe size-only breadth in radians along the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naxis. For the explicitly assumed Gaussian profile convolution,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_s=\\sqrt{\\beta_{\\mathrm{obs}}^2-\\beta_{\\mathrm{inst}}^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nquadrature subtraction is not universal for Lorentzian or Voigt peaks.\nMicrostrain is excluded from this illustration.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\frac{K_s\\lambda}{\\beta_s\\cos\\theta},\\qquad \\beta_s=\\sqrt{\\beta_{\\mathrm{obs}}^2-\\beta_{\\mathrm{inst}}^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.154<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.154\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_s=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\npeak center\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>40<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\theta=40^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nobserved FWHM\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>0.30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand instrumental FWHM\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAssuming Gaussian profiles gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0.2828<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.9365<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_s=0.2828^\\circ=4.9365\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>20<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=20^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe estimated coherent-domain size is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>29.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\approx29.9\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe image shows a constructed peak and separate widths, not actual\ndiffraction measurements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_322_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic Gaussian profiles showing observed, instrumental and size-related peak widths.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic Gaussian profiles showing\nobserved, instrumental and size-related peak widths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Scherrer example with Gaussian instrumental correction; not\nsample data.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen a reflection is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>60<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\theta=60^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is it inappropriate to call\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe particle diameter without qualification?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The Scherrer estimate halves.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It characterizes coherent scattering domains and depends on line\nshape, microstrain and instrumental correction.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Application requires a known wavelength, adequate peak profiles and a\njustified broadening model. Width corrections must use matching angle\nconventions and units; instrument-dominated peaks yield unstable\nestimates. Reliable materials characterization often requires multiple\nreflections and complementary techniques.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Under the assumed Gaussian model, the Scherrer equation estimates the\nsize of coherently diffracting domains from an instrument-corrected\nX-ray peak width. Peak shape and possible microstrain limit this\nestimate; the crystallite size is not automatically the particle\ndiameter. The module connects solid-state models with experimental\nmaterials characterization.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://journals.iucr.org/j/issues/2024/05/00/oc5037/index.html\">IUCr Journal of Applied Crystallography – Determination of the Average Crystallite Size<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Scherrer formula, shape factor and instrument profile; crystallite versus particle; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L8 – X-Ray Diffraction, Line Broadening and Scherrer Estimation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://journals.iucr.org/s/issues/2017/03/00/ie5175/\">IUCr – Understanding the Instrumental Profile of Synchrotron Radiation X-ray Powder Diffraction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Instrumental and sample broadening; line shape and microstrain need independent treatment; Subject relevance to lesson: M35-L8 – X-Ray Diffraction, Line Broadening and Scherrer Estimation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
